 "prompt_text": "[gMASK]<sop><|user|>Explain how a heat pump moves heat from a cold place to a warm one, and why its efficiency falls in very cold weather.<|assistant|><think></think>",
 "completion_text": "# How Heat Pumps Work\n\n## The Basic Principle\n\nA heat pump doesn't *create* heat\u2014it moves existing heat from one place to another, using a refrigeration cycle run in reverse. This is possible because heat naturally exists even in cold air (down to absolute zero, \u2212273\u00b0C), and a heat pump can extract some of it.\n\n## The Four-Step Cycle\n\n**1. Evaporation (heat absorption)**\nA refrigerant\u2014a fluid with a very low boiling point\u2014flows through coils in the cold environment (outside air, ground, or water). Even when it's freezing outside, the refrigerant is colder still, so heat flows into it, causing the refrigerant to boil and evaporate into a gas.\n\n**2. Compression**\nAn electric compressor squeezes the gas, dramatically increasing its pressure and temperature. This is where the pump does work on the system\u2014the compressed gas can now be much hotter than your home.\n\n**3. Condensation (heat release)**\nThe hot, high-pressure gas flows through coils inside your home. Since it's hotter than the indoor air, heat flows out into your rooms. As it loses heat, the gas condenses back into a liquid, releasing its latent heat.\n\n**4. Expansion**\nThe liquid passes through an expansion valve, dropping its pressure and temperature sharply. It's now cold enough to absorb heat from the outside again, and the cycle repeats.\n\n## Why Efficiency Drops in Cold Weather\n\n**1. Smaller temperature difference to harvest**\nHeat transfer requires a temperature *difference*. As outdoor temperatures fall, there's less thermal energy available, and the refrigerant must be colder to absorb it. The gap between the refrigerant and the outside air shrinks, slowing heat absorption.\n\n**2. Larger lift required**\nThe compressor must raise the refrigerant from a lower starting temperature to the same indoor temperature. A bigger temperature \"lift",
